  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 221 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper-level ridge is over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana this afternoon. This will allow temperatures \nto warm up this afternoon. Monsoonal moisture is beginning to work\nits way in over the area this afternoon. This will bring a few \nisolated showers and thunderstorms to North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana this afternoon. On Wednesday the upper-level \nridge remains over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. An upper-level shortwave will move through the upper- \nlevel flow and bring showers and thunderstorms primarily south of \nI-90 Wednesday afternoon and evening. Due to an increase in \nmonsoonal moisture the thunderstorms will produce heavy rainfall. \nThere is a lack of steering flow so the thunderstorms will also be\nslow moving. This has the potential to lead to some localized \nflooding. See the Hydrology section for details. On Wednesday \nisolated showers and thunderstorms have the potential to make it \nfurther north but stay south of an Augusta to Great Falls to \nLewistown line. \n\nOn Thursday the upper-level ridge remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. Another upper-level shortwave \nmove through the flow. This will bring another day of hot weather \nwith isolated showers and thunderstorms. On Friday the upper-level \nridge remains over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nThis will increase temperatures to well-above seasonal averages. \nWinds will increase due to low-level and mid-level winds increasing. \nThis will dry out the area and bring elevated fire weather \nconditions to North-central Montana. On Saturday the upper-level \nridge remains over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. It will be the hottest out of this forecast period across \nthe area on Saturday with well-above seasonal average temperatures. \nOn Sunday a weak upper-level trough moves through which will \nslightly cool down temperatures and bring some windy conditions to \nthe area. On Monday the upper-level ridge remains over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern. This will allow warm and dry \nconditions to persist. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Wednesday there is a 40 - 60% chance for thunderstorms across \nSouthwestern Montana. On Wednesday there is a 15 - 35% chance for \nthunderstorms across most of North-central and Central Montana. On \nThursday there is a 20 - 40% chance for thunderstorms across North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On Friday there is a 25 -\n 30% chance for thunderstorms across Southwestern Montana. On \nSaturday there is a 25 - 40% chance for thunderstorms across \nSouthwestern Montana.\n\nOn Friday there is a 40 - 80% chance for high temperatures of 100 \ndegrees or greater across most of North-central Montana. On Saturday \nthere is greater than a 60% chance for high temperatures of 100 \ndegrees or greater across North-central Montana. On Saturday \nthere is a 30 - 50% chance for high temperatures of 100 degrees or\ngreater from the Helena Valley through the Canyon Ferry area to \nTownsend to the Three Forks to Bozeman area. -IG",
